<!--{"title": "Securing MCP servers for enterprise AI agents: a checklist", "description": "A practical checklist for running Model Context Protocol servers in production: token audience, confused deputy, least privilege, gateways, logging and prompt injection.", "h1": "Securing MCP servers for enterprise AI agents: an architect's checklist", "kicker": "AI architecture", "lede": "MCP makes it easy to give agents real tools. That is exactly why the security model needs as much attention as the prompts. This is the checklist I work through before an MCP server touches production data.", "date": "2026-10-17", "datetime": "2026-10-17T18:55:00+04:00", "readtime": "9 min read", "format": "checklist", "tags": ["ai", "agents", "mcp", "security"], "keywords": ["Model Context Protocol security", "MCP server security", "MCP authorization", "AI agent security", "confused deputy MCP", "token passthrough", "OAuth resource indicators RFC 8707", "prompt injection agents", "enterprise AI agents", "AI gateway", "LLM security checklist"], "faq": [["What is token passthrough in MCP and why is it banned?", "Token passthrough is when an MCP server accepts a token from a client and forwards it to a downstream API without checking it was issued for the MCP server. The MCP security guidance says servers must not accept tokens that were not explicitly issued for them, because it breaks audience checks and audit trails."], ["What is the confused deputy problem with MCP proxies?", "It happens when an MCP proxy uses one static client ID with a third-party authorisation server that remembers consent per client rather than per user, so a malicious client can obtain authorisation codes without the user's real consent. The fix is per-client consent at the proxy and strict redirect URI validation."], ["Do I need an AI gateway in front of MCP servers?", "For enterprise use I recommend one. A single governed entry point gives you authentication, least-privilege scopes, rate limits, PII redaction and a